WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.460
Into the early nineteenth century, many chemists thought the molecules of living

2
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:09.500
things needed living things to make them.

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.900
Sugar, fat, urea: all of it was supposed to carry a vital force.

4
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.420
The chemist Berzelius drew the line.

5
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.920
Inorganic matter, the chemistry of rocks and salts, could be made in the lab.

6
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.320
Organic matter, the chemistry of living bodies, needed life to make it.

7
00:00:26.283 --> 00:00:31.563
In February 1828, Friedrich Wöhler tried to make an inorganic salt, ammonium

8
00:00:31.563 --> 00:00:36.503
cyanate. What crystallized in his flask was urea, the molecule the body makes to

9
00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:37.503
dispose of nitrogen.

10
00:00:38.103 --> 00:00:39.423
There was no kidney in the room.

11
00:00:40.063 --> 00:00:45.223
Both compounds have the same formula: one carbon, four hydrogens, two nitrogens, one

12
00:00:45.223 --> 00:00:48.203
oxygen. Same atoms, connected differently.

13
00:00:48.943 --> 00:00:50.303
Heat rearranged the connections.

14
00:00:51.433 --> 00:00:54.573
Wöhler's flask did not kill vitalism that afternoon.

15
00:00:55.373 --> 00:00:59.453
Chemists saw a puzzle, not a refutation, though skeptics could trace the cyanate to

16
00:00:59.453 --> 00:01:03.853
organic sources. The vital force pulled back, but it had somewhere to stand.

17
00:01:04.553 --> 00:01:10.173
In 1845 Hermann Kolbe built acetic acid from carbon disulfide, made by heating

18
00:01:10.173 --> 00:01:13.033
charcoal with sulfur, with nothing alive in the chain.

19
00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:18.193
A decade later Marcellin Berthelot built fats from glycerol and fatty acids.

20
00:01:19.217 --> 00:01:20.577
One redoubt remained.

21
00:01:21.397 --> 00:01:24.417
Louis Pasteur had proved that fermentation depends on living yeast.

22
00:01:25.157 --> 00:01:29.697
From this he drew a stronger line than his data required: that fermentation was

23
00:01:29.697 --> 00:01:31.757
inseparable from the intact living cell.

24
00:01:32.417 --> 00:01:34.277
That argument stayed open for decades.

25
00:01:35.017 --> 00:01:40.877
In 1877 Wilhelm Kühne proposed the word enzyme, from the Greek for in leaven, for

26
00:01:40.877 --> 00:01:43.357
soluble ferments like pepsin and invertase.

27
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.320
In 1896 Eduard Buchner ground brewer's yeast with sand and pressed out a clear

28
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.780
golden juice with almost no intact cells in it.

29
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.700
He added sugar to preserve it.

30
00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.500
The sugar began to ferment, carbon dioxide rising through a flask of dead yeast.

31
00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.280
Vitalists said living scraps must have survived.

32
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.320
Buchner ran two controls.

33
00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.720
Juice with chloroform, an antiseptic, still fermented.

34
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.640
So did juice through a sterile filter, so no cells were needed.

35
00:02:11.683 --> 00:02:16.683
Seventy years of syntheses made the vital force unnecessary, one molecule and one

36
00:02:16.683 --> 00:02:17.703
process at a time.

37
00:02:18.363 --> 00:02:22.683
What replaced it is the premise of this course: a living cell obeys the same

38
00:02:22.683 --> 00:02:26.363
chemistry as everything else, with no separate law of its own.

39
00:02:27.083 --> 00:02:32.163
The catalysts Kühne named and Buchner freed are, nearly all, ordinary proteins.
